# text = It is true that Grandcourt went about with the sense that he did not care a languid curse for any one's admiration : but this state of not-caring , just as much as desire , required its related object -- namely , a world of admiring or envying spectators : for if you are fond of looking stonily at smiling persons -- the persons must be and they must smile -- a rudimentary truth which is surely forgotten by those who complain of mankind as generally contemptible , since any other aspect of the race must disappoint the voracity of their contempt .
